 This is the source code of [Fizzy](https://fizzy.do/), the Kanban tracking tool for issues and ideas by [37signals](https://37signals.com).
 
 
+## Deploy
+
+To run Fizzy on one Linux server (Docker, SQLite, jobs in the same container, TLS from kamal-proxy), fill in the hostname, mail server, and secrets in [`config/deploy.yml`](config/deploy.yml) and [`.kamal/secrets`](.kamal/secrets), then run `kamal setup`. See the [deployment guide](docs/deployment.md).
+
 ## Development
 
 You are welcome -- and encouraged -- to modify Fizzy to your liking.
diff --git a/config/deploy.yml b/config/deploy.yml
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..9a12f9d
--- /dev/null
+++ b/config/deploy.yml
@@ -0,0 +1,107 @@
+# One-box Kamal config for Fizzy.
+#
+# A Linux server, Docker, SQLite, web and background jobs in one container,
+# TLS from kamal-proxy. Data lives on a Docker volume, so it survives the next deploy.
+#
+# Fill in the two values below and the secrets in .kamal/secrets, point DNS at
+# the box (ports 80 and 443 open), then from this directory:
+#
+#   kamal setup
+#
+# Fizzy will be at https://<host>. Later deploys are `kamal deploy`.
+#
+# `x-` keys are ignored by Kamal; they exist so the hostname and mail server
+# are filled in once and reused below.
+
+# Public hostname. DNS must point here. Also the SSH target unless you split it
+# (see servers below).
+x-host: &host fizzy.example.com
+
+# SMTP server your mail is sent through. Magic-link login needs this.
+x-mail-server: &mail_server smtp.example.com
+
+# Address recipients see. Must be allowed by the mail server.
+x-mail-from: &mail_from "Fizzy <fizzy@example.com>"
+
+service: fizzy
+
+# With the local registry below, the image name is just the service name.
+# Switch this if you push to Docker Hub or GHCR (your-user/fizzy, ghcr.io/your-user/fizzy).
+image: fizzy
+
+servers:
+  web:
+    # One host, one app container. Jobs run inside it (see SOLID_QUEUE_IN_PUMA);
+    # do not add a jobs role or they will not see this container's SQLite files.
+    #
+    # Replace *host with an IP here only if SSH is not the public hostname.
+    - *host
+
+# kamal-proxy terminates TLS (Let's Encrypt) and forwards to Thruster on port 80.
+# production.rb already assumes SSL from that proxy.
+proxy:
+  ssl: true
+  host: *host
+  app_port: 80
+  healthcheck:
+    path: /up
+
+# No registry account required. Kamal runs a local registry and the server pulls
+# through SSH. To use Docker Hub or GHCR instead:
+#
+#   registry:
+#     server: ghcr.io
+#     username: your-user
+#     password:
+#       - KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD
+#
+# and set image to match. Add KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD to .kamal/secrets.
+registry:
+  server: localhost:5555
+
+env:
+  clear:
+    APP_HOST: *host
+    SMTP_ADDRESS: *mail_server
+    SMTP_PORT: "587"
+    MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS: *mail_from
+
+    # SQLite on the volume below. Do not point this at MySQL on the one-box setup.
+    DATABASE_ADAPTER: sqlite
+    ACTIVE_STORAGE_SERVICE: local
+
+    # Solid Queue supervisor inside Puma, so background jobs run in this container.
+    # config/puma.rb already does this for the open-source app; keep it explicit.
+    SOLID_QUEUE_IN_PUMA: "true"
+
+    # Uncomment on a small box if you want fewer Puma workers than CPUs.
+    # WEB_CONCURRENCY: 1
+    # JOB_CONCURRENCY: 1
+  secret:
+    - SECRET_KEY_BASE
+    - SMTP_USERNAME
+    - SMTP_PASSWORD
+    # Optional web push. Generate with WebPush.generate_key and uncomment both
+    # here and in .kamal/secrets.
+    # - VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY
+    # - VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY
+
+# Named volume: SQLite (primary, queue, cache, cable) and local Active Storage.
+# Survives container replacement on the next deploy. Do not change the container
+# path; the databases are written under /rails/storage.
+volumes:
+  - fizzy_storage:/rails/storage
+
+asset_path: /rails/public/assets
+
+builder:
+  # Match a typical Linux VPS. Use arm64 for Graviton or a Raspberry Pi.
+  arch: amd64
+
+aliases:
+  console: app exec --interactive --reuse "bin/rails console"
+  shell: app exec --interactive --reuse "bash"
+  logs: app logs -f
+
+# ssh:
+#   user: app
